            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes an array of values from start up to, but not including, stop, going up by step: np.arange 5 is 0 1 2 3 4, np.arange 0 1 0.25 is 0 0.25 0.5 0.75.\n\nIt fixes the STEP and lets the count follow. np.linspace does the opposite: it fixes the count and both ends, and works out the step. Use np.arange for indices (0 to N-1), sample times at a known rate, or anything where the spacing matters more than the exact end.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.arange <stop>\nnp.arange <start> <stop> <step>\nDefaults: start 0, stop 10, step 1. Whole-number arguments make whole-number\nvalues; a decimal anywhere makes decimals.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.arange 64\nnp.arange 0 2 0.1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the array and sends it.\n\nstart (number box, also an inlet):\nThe first value.\n\nstop (number box, also an inlet):\nWhere to stop: values reach up to it but never include it.\n\nstep (number box, also an inlet):\nThe step between values. A negative step counts down (start must then be above stop). 0 makes nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narange out:\nThe array.\n\nTHE END IS LEFT OUT:\nnp.arange 0 1 0.25 stops at 0.75, not 1. With decimal steps the number of values can also come out one more or fewer than expected because of rounding; when the exact count matters, use np.linspace.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nClicked at load: np.arange 5 gives 0 1 2 3 4, and np.arange 0 1 0.25 gives 0 0.25 0.5 0.75 - no 1. Click a button to send again.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linspace fixes the count instead of the step. t.arange is the PyTorch version. range_counter counts in a stream, one number at a time.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
